% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 4
% composition-elements: D minor (one flat) with a C sharp accidental at the two cadences; intervals by step, skip and leap (up to a fifth); a fermata on the final note; p, mf and f with crescendo and diminuendo hairpins; a soloist's phrase, its reshaping and an ensemble answer in three four-bar phrases
% composition-listen: Umm Kulthum — "Alf Leila wa Leila" (words by Morsi Gamil Aziz, music by Baligh Hamdi), first performed in 1969
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Umm+Kulthum+Alf+Leila+wa+Leila+1969
% composition-bridge: Hear how a singer reshapes a line, how the ensemble answers, and how expectation can build across a long performance.
% composition-context: Umm Kulthum (c. 1904-1975) was Egypt's most celebrated singer; her monthly concerts, broadcast across the Arab world, stretched one song over an hour as she reshaped each line in answer to her listeners. That practice can introduce Arabic art music, maqam (here maqam Farahfaza, related to Nahawand) and tarab, the shared state of musical rapture. This short equal-tempered original is not a maqam composition.
% composition-distance: Original pitches and no Arabic text; no phrase, rhythm or orchestration of the song is copied. The first draft's E flat over D, a stock "Middle Eastern" signifier, was removed; the only accidental is a C sharp leading note, and the piece claims no maqam.
