% dossier-function: original Grade 4 instrumental expressive study and listening bridge; a soloist states a phrase and reshapes it, the ensemble answers and rises to a climax, and the line settles to a held cadence, before the class listens to how Umm Kulthum and her orchestra stretch and answer a line
% dossier-elements: twelve bars in 4/4. Phrase 1 (bars 1-4, soloist): D4-F4 quarters, A4 half | G4-F4-E4-F4 eighths, D4 half | D4-F4-A4-D5 quarters | C5-B flat 4-A4-G4 eighths, A4 half. Phrase 2 (bars 5-8, ensemble): F4-G4-A4-F4 | E4-D4-C sharp 4-D4 | crescendo A4-C5-D5-E5 | f, D5-C5 quarters, A4 half. Phrase 3 (bars 9-12): diminuendo B flat 4-A4-G4-F4 | E4-F4 quarters, G4 half | A4-G4-F4-E4 | p, C sharp 4 quarter, D4 dotted half with fermata. D natural minor with a C sharp leading note at bars 6 and 12; written range C sharp 4-E5; eighths, quarters, halves, one dotted half
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 66 is the target; begin at 60 and let the soloist breathe at the end of bars 2 and 4. D minor (one flat) is the rehearsal key and the written range C sharp 4-E5 suits Grade 4 voices on "loo" and sits a semitone below and a step above the young-unison C4-D5 range; on soprano recorder the low C sharp needs a half-covered bottom hole, so recorders may take bars 5-12 with voices or bells on bars 6 and 12. Teach in steps: sing phrase 1 on "loo", find the C sharp on the staff and on bells, add the hairpins, then choose a soloist for bars 1-4
% dossier-accompaniment: The page's ballad style (soft piano and bass) gives a neutral background; it is deliberately not the page's drone or Persian style, which would suggest a tradition this piece does not belong to. Do not add darbuka, riq or oud imitations; the piece is a Western-notated expressive study
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental: no words are sung. Grade 4 may sing the line on a neutral syllable ("loo") or play it on recorder, bells, xylophone (with a C sharp bar) or keyboard. No Arabic text is used or imitated
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original course composition made for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, grade and form constraints. It is not traditional, not Arabic repertoire, not a maqam demonstration and not modelled on any phrase of "Alf Leila wa Leila"; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. Umm Kulthum's recorded performance and the underlying composition remain the copyright of their owners where applicable and are not included
% dossier-respect: Centre Umm Kulthum, the poet Morsi Gamil Aziz and the composer Baligh Hamdi, and Arabic listening practices: long improvised extensions, the audience's responses and tarab. Explain that staff notation and twelve-tone classroom instruments omit maqam intervals and ornaments, and never present this piece or its minor key as an Arabic sound
% dossier-review: Arab music educator, Grade 4 teacher and second score reader; all reviews still required
