N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals eight eighths with no pickup. Bar 1 D4-F4 quarters, A4 half; bar 2 G4-F4-E4-F4 eighths, D4 half; bar 3 D4-F4-A4-D5 quarters; bar 4 C5-B flat 4-A4-G4 eighths, A4 half; bar 5 F4-G4-A4-F4 quarters; bar 6 E4-D4-C sharp 4-D4 quarters; bar 7 A4-C5-D5-E5 quarters; bar 8 D5-C5 quarters, A4 half; bar 9 B flat 4-A4-G4-F4 quarters; bar 10 E4-F4 quarters, G4 half; bar 11 A4-G4-F4-E4 quarters; bar 12 C sharp 4 quarter, D4 dotted half under a fermata. D natural minor plus C sharp, range C sharp 4-E5, largest leap a fifth (D4 up to A4 into bar 7), no syncopation, dotted pairs or ties. Form: statement and reshaping (1-4), ensemble answer rising to the f climax (5-8), descent to the held cadence (9-12). The first draft's E flat (a flattened second over D) was replaced because it reads as a "Middle Eastern" cliché. Originality check: compared with "Alf Leila wa Leila" (no phrase used) and with common minor-key school and folk tunes (Hava Nagila, Erev Shel Shoshanim, Scarborough Fair, Greensleeves, Shalom Chaverim); the D-F-A arpeggio opening is common property and the line matches none of them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 4 players shape the hairpins and the fermata together, whether the low C sharp is practical on recorder, and how much reshaping a soloist can manage in bar 4 need a classroom trial. The 1969 recording's label and date of first release were not verified; an Arab music educator should confirm the framing of maqam and tarab and the description of Umm Kulthum's concert practice.
